      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the fourth term of the sequence where consecutive powers of 3 are added. The sequence starts with \\(3^0\\), followed by \\(3^0 + 3^1\\), then \\(3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2\\), and so on. We need to find the fourth term of this sequence.\n\nThe general form of the \\(n\\)-th term of the sequence is the sum of the first \\(n\\) powers of 3. Therefore, the fourth term of the sequence is the sum of the first 4 powers of 3:\n\\[\n3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2 + 3^3\n\\]\n\nWe will calculate each term individually:\n\\[\n3^0 = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n3^1 = 3\n\\]\n\\[\n3^2 = 9\n\\]\n\\[\n3^3 = 27\n\\]\n\nNow, we add these values together:\n\\[\n3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2 + 3^3 = 1 + 3 + 9 + 27\n\\]\n\nWe can add these step by step:\n\\[\n1 + 3 = 4\n\\]\n\\[\n4 + 9 = 13\n\\]\n\\[\n13 + 27 = 40\n\\]\n\nThus, the fourth term of the sequence is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
